---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = "/use-cases/analytics";
const title = "Shopify email analytics: build a report you can reconcile";
const desc = "Create an operational email reporting workflow with explicit units, order reconciliation, cohort maturity and accountable decisions.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Why do platform and store revenue differ?",
    "answer": "They may use different attribution windows, order definitions, timezones, refund cutoffs or identity matching. Reconcile those definitions and inspect order-level links before treating the gap as an error."
  },
  {
    "question": "What is revenue per recipient?",
    "answer": "Define it as revenue under a named attribution rule divided by unique recipients in the stated population and window. Revenue per delivered message is a separate metric; label both explicitly."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does cohort analysis prove lift?",
    "answer": "No. Cohorts describe observed behavior and need equal follow-up ages. A causal claim requires a suitable comparison design and an account of uncertainty and contamination."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <p class="editorial-kicker">Shopify email operating library</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9">An email dashboard becomes useful when another operator can reproduce its numbers and identify the action they support. This workflow builds a recurring report from message events and orders. It does not claim supplier tests, merchant gains or a universal attribution model.</p>
  </section>
  <article class="container prose-shopify pb-16">
    <section class="card mb-8"><h2>Give the report a decision and an owner</h2>
      <p>Choose what the report must answer: did delivery fail, did an offer reduce contribution, or did a workflow’s audience change? Assign a reporting owner, a source owner for each feed and a finance reviewer for monetary definitions. Set review frequency around volume, refund maturity and operational risk.</p><p>Keep an operational alert view separate from a mature commercial view. A fresh send can expose a broken link today while its eventual returns and repeat purchases remain unknown. Do not wait for a completed revenue window to investigate a sending defect.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="card mb-8"><h2>Write the metric dictionary first</h2>
      <p>Record unit, numerator, denominator, time basis, timezone, currency, source and exclusions for every column. Revenue per delivered message and revenue per unique recipient answer different questions. A person receiving three touches contributes three messages but one unique recipient within a defined reporting window.</p><p>Define attempted, accepted, delivered and bounced using the source’s event semantics. Delivery does not establish inbox placement. Define complaint and unsubscribe rates against a named population, and retain counts so a small denominator does not disappear behind a percentage.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="card mb-8"><h2>Keep separate message, attribution and order tables</h2>
      <p>Use stable message, recipient, campaign and workflow-version identifiers in the send ledger. Record source event timestamps and ingestion timestamps. Keep attribution links in a separate table with model, lookback window and credited value; keep orders and refunds at a documented order or line-item grain.</p><p>An order may link to several messages. Joining those tables directly and summing order value multiplies revenue. Deduplicate order IDs for an order-total view or allocate explicit credit for a message-credit view. Do not merge the two totals under one label.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="card mb-8"><h2>Reconcile a closed period before interpreting trends</h2>
      <p>Save an extraction cutoff and source snapshots. Compare counts of orders, refunds and message IDs with each source. Check missing keys, duplicate rows, changed workflow names, currency conversions and late-arriving events. Use stable IDs rather than customer email as the primary order join when those IDs are available.</p><p>Reconcile platform credit to store orders by explaining model, timezone, tax, shipping and window differences. Matching totals are not required when definitions differ, but the difference needs a traceable explanation. UTMs help identify visits; they do not repair every identity gap or establish causation.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="card mb-8"><h2>Measure contribution without counting costs twice</h2>
      <p>Start with net merchandise revenue after discounts and refunds. Deduct product cost and relevant variable payment, fulfillment and shipping subsidy costs to obtain the chosen contribution measure. State the treatment of tax and shipping revenue. Discount codes are diagnostic fields, not a second expense when the net sales basis already includes them.</p><p>Report channel fees, platform subscriptions and labor separately with an explicit allocation method if assigning them to flows. A partially allocated contribution report is not full program ROI. Use <a href="/guides/pricing-models">pricing models</a> for the total cost forecast.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="card mb-8"><h2>Make cohort comparisons mature and descriptive</h2>
      <p>Choose a cohort anchor such as first paid order or first permissioned signup, then compare equal follow-up ages. A recent signup cohort cannot yet have the same observation horizon as an older one. Display incomplete cohorts as immature rather than recording missing future revenue as zero.</p><p>Separate subscription renewals, new subscription starts, wholesale orders and gift contexts when they change interpretation. Engagement-based groups can differ before any message is sent; stronger repeat purchase among clickers does not prove the email caused it. Keep that limitation attached to the chart or table.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="card mb-8"><h2>Use diagnostics to find the broken step</h2>
      <p>When revenue per recipient falls, inspect audience mix, reachability, delivery, click behavior, order linkage, offer and refunds in sequence. Check tracking changes before rewriting copy. Use opens as a limited diagnostic because automated activity and privacy behavior can affect them; they are not a sufficient trigger for a commercial decision.</p><p>Compare to a relevant internal baseline with counts and uncertainty. A flat store total alongside rising email credit can reflect model changes, channel mix or real behavior; it does not by itself prove inflated attribution. Investigate the ledger before drawing that conclusion.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="card mb-8"><h2>Keep causal measurement in its own record</h2>
      <p>A holdout needs a stable assignment unit, explicit eligibility, a primary outcome, a follow-up horizon and a plan for contamination across channels. Size and duration depend on event volume, variability and the effect worth detecting; a fixed percentage or monthly calendar is not a guarantee of useful evidence.</p><p>Report outcomes per assigned unit with uncertainty and record deviations from assignment. An inconclusive comparison is not proof of no effect. The <a href="/guides/revenue-attribution">revenue attribution guide</a> owns experiment design; this report stores assignment metadata and links to its results without relabeling ordinary attribution as lift.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="card mb-8"><h2>Rehearse the reporting pipeline</h2>
      <p>Proposed non-sending fixture: one order credited to two messages, a refunded line, a delayed event, a repeated webhook, a missing customer ID, a foreign-currency order and a recurring subscription charge. Specify expected row counts and sums before importing the fixture. Confirm the report exposes missing mappings instead of silently dropping them.</p><p>Then reproduce one closed report from the saved inputs. Check that the same definitions yield the same counts and that corrections create a documented revision. Evaluate candidate apps on export grain, identifiers, model visibility and reproducibility rather than the number of dashboard panels.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="card mb-8"><h2>Publish decisions with their limitations</h2>
      <p>Each review should record the anomaly, evidence, chosen action, owner and next observation point. Link to the workflow version and the report cutoff. Correct a confirmed purchase-suppression defect promptly; reserve causal claims about a new discount for the appropriate comparison.</p><p><a href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">Cart recovery</a> supplies cart eligibility and state definitions; <a href="/guides/segmentation">segmentation</a> supplies audience predicates; this page owns the repeatable reporting handoff. Keep unresolved source gaps visible and avoid turning one attractive metric into a universal app ranking.</p>
    </section>
  </article>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</BaseLayout>
